    {
      "id": "Ugxoo5AtZm0eZcXtXdV4AaABAg",
      "author": "@TerenceClark",
      "authorImage": "https://yt3.ggpht.com/ytc/AIdro_katNyt2kD1uR5yF5CL5S5HtVXd8T-VKHuoNfdfcVhFSrEC=s48-c-k-c0x00ffffff-no-rj",
      "text": "I had an Uber trip with 3 Surinamese passengers. As a language nerd it was fascinating! I didn't even realize they were speaking Dutch at first because of their dialect (also I don't speak Dutch, but I can usually ID it). When they found out I loved language and culture they spoke some Bahasa Jawa and Sranan Tongo. I don't usually press people to speak their languages because it can sometimes seem a bit rude come on but they were excited to share. \n\nTo the diversity comment I can at least anecdotally attest. The only language they all spoke was Dutch because one of them was ethnically Javanese and another was mostly ethnically native Surinamese and spoke mostly Sranan Tongo growing up. Sranan Tongo is its own wild language as it's fundamentally an English creole, but was mostly cut off from English speaking countries for hundreds of years, so their English substrate is more like early modern to middle modern English. Plus, of course, massive influence from all of the diversity in the country.",
      "likeCount": 0,
      "publishedAt": "2026-08-28T12:31:09Z",
      "replyCount": 0,
      "replies": []
    },
